    "turn_text": "they've got to pick their own folks and make decisions on how they keep bending to the extremists and their party. That's how they've decided to do this and, and again, empower them. But again, you know, there was a lot of outreach and we tried to find a way forward together. Speaker McCarthy made it clear in his press conference, I think he said I'm not going to sell my soul or something like that, referring to working with Democrats on something that we could encourage more bipartisan governing and working together. That's totally his decision, and that's up for him to make, and was up for him to make. And now the question is, what do we do going forward? Because to me, that's what the country wants us to do. How are you going to make this work? We've got to figure out ways, and then maybe looking at the rules to change the rules and change the motion of AK so it's not just one person being able to throw out a speaker. That's a set of rules that we change as the problem-sauce caucus in prior Congress that changed back this year. And I don't think it was a smart move. But I understand Matt Gaetz wanted it, but you're just giving them a loaded weapon when you do that. But it's going to take also making sure we change the rules to encourage more bipartisan governing, more democratic bills being able to get to the floor where They're both sides can agree. More amendments from Democrats, encouraging a more bipartisan house.",
